> Two pages is already an appropriate length; I would extend only enough to sharpen **independence, trajectory, and fit**, rather than add more project inventory.
> 
> The strongest additions would be:
> 
> - **Explicit intellectual independence.** Give one concrete example of a question, method, or analytical solution Tahmid originated—not merely executed. The trade-spillover work is ideal for this.
> - **Why Michigan Society of Fellows specifically.** Explain why his work will benefit from—and contribute to—a cross-disciplinary community spanning economics, environment, public health, policy, and area studies. This is probably the largest current omission.
> - **Comparative assessment.** Add a calibrated statement such as “among the top X% of students I have advised/worked with,” if you can honestly support it. Fellowship committees value this signal.
> - **Research trajectory beyond the dissertation.** Briefly frame *Beyond the Cash Wage* as the beginning of a durable research program on climate, labor, and welfare in vulnerable agricultural economies.
> - **Collaborative maturity.** You describe service well, but could emphasize that he can work across disciplinary and institutional boundaries—e.g., with state agencies, international assessment teams, and survey respondents/partners.
> - **A short point on scholarly judgment.** For instance, his ability to recognize limitations in available data, revise specifications, and connect quantitative findings to institutional realities.
> 
> You could add a paragraph before the conclusion like:
> 
> > Tahmid is especially well suited to the Society of Fellows because he is not confined by conventional disciplinary boundaries. His work brings together environmental economics, climate-impact modeling, public health, agricultural systems, and applied data science, while remaining grounded in the lived conditions facing workers and farmers. I expect his proposed project to establish a broader long-term research program on climate vulnerability, labor institutions, and adaptation in lower-income countries. He would contribute not only technical skill but also intellectual generosity, practical judgment, and a genuine commitment to research that improves human well-being.
> 
> I would also tighten rather than extend a few passages—for example, the DNREC, Nature, and CCAC sections could each gain one sentence explicitly stating their real-world or scholarly significance. That would make the letter feel more substantial without becoming longer merely for length’s sake.
